{"tool_name":"fastseg","contents":["bioconda","bioconductor","biotools","oeb"],"identifiers":["biotools:fastseg","bioconda:bioconductor-fastseg","bioconductor:fastseg"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"bioconductor-fastseg","version":"1.56.0","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/html/fastseg.html","license":"LGPL (>= 2.0)","summary":"fastseg - a fast segmentation algorithm","identifiers":["biotools:fastseg"],"description":"fastseg implements a very fast and efficient segmentation algorithm. It has similar functionality as DNACopy (Olshen and Venkatraman 2004), but is considerably faster and more flexible. fastseg can segment data from DNA microarrays and data from next generation sequencing for example to detect copy number segments. Further it can segment data from RNA microarrays like tiling arrays to identify transcripts. Most generally, it can segment data given as a matrix or as a vector. Various data formats can be used as input to fastseg like expression set objects for microarrays or GRanges for sequencing data. The segmentation criterion of fastseg is based on a statistical test in a Bayesian framework, namely the cyber t-test (Baldi 2001). The speed-up arises from the facts, that sampling is not necessary in for fastseg and that a dynamic programming approach is used for calculation of the segments' first and higher order moments.","license_file":"/lib/R/share/licenses/LGPL-3","build":{"number":0,"rpaths":["lib/R/lib/","lib/"],"run_exports":""},"requirements":{"build":[null,null,"make"],"host":["bioconductor-biobase >=2.70.0,<2.71.0","bioconductor-biocgenerics >=0.56.0,<0.57.0","bioconductor-genomicranges >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-iranges >=2.44.0,<2.45.0","bioconductor-s4vectors >=0.48.0,<0.49.0","r-base","libblas","liblapack","zlib","liblzma-devel"],"run":["bioconductor-biobase >=2.70.0,<2.71.0","bioconductor-biocgenerics >=0.56.0,<0.57.0","bioconductor-genomicranges >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-iranges >=2.44.0,<2.45.0","bioconductor-s4vectors >=0.48.0,<0.49.0","r-base"]},"source_url":["https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/fastseg_1.56.0.tar.gz","https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/Archive/fastseg/fastseg_1.56.0.tar.gz","https://bioarchive.galaxyproject.org/fastseg_1.56.0.tar.gz","https://depot.galaxyproject.org/software/bioconductor-fastseg/bioconductor-fastseg_1.56.0_src_all.tar.gz"],"test":{"commands":["$R -e \"library('fastseg')\""]},"parent_recipe":{"name":"bioconductor-fastseg","path":"recipes/bioconductor-fastseg","version":"1.26.0"}},"biotools":{"id":"fastseg","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/cn.mops","license":"LGPL-2.0-or-later","summary":"fastseg implements a very fast and efficient segmentation algorithm. It has similar functionality as DNACopy (Olshen and Venkatraman 2004), but is considerably faster and more flexible. fastseg can segment data from DNA microarrays and data from next generation sequencing for example to detect copy number segments. Further it can segment data from RNA microarrays like tiling arrays to identify transcripts. Most generally, it can segment data given as a matrix or as a vector. Various data formats can be used as input to fastseg like expression set objects for microarrays or GRanges for sequencing data. The segmentation criterion of fastseg is based on a statistical test in a Bayesian framework, namely the cyber t-test (Baldi 2001). The speed-up arises from the facts, that sampling is not necessary in for fastseg and that a dynamic programming approach is used for calculation of the segments' first and higher order moments.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T15:00:14Z","last_update_date":"2026-11-22T21:27:43.057050Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["1.58.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:fastseg","credit":[{"name":"Guenter Klambauer","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"name":"Gundula Povysil","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Maintainer"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/cn.mops"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2403"}]}],"name":"fastseg","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gks003","metadata":{"abstract":"Quantitative analyses of next-generation sequencing (NGS) data, such as the detection of copy number variations (CNVs), remain challenging. Current methods detect CNVs as changes in the depth of coverage along chromosomes. Technological or genomic variations in the depth of coverage thus lead to a high false discovery rate (FDR), even upon correction for GC content. In the context of association studies between CNVs and disease, a high FDR means many false CNVs, thereby decreasing the discovery power of the study after correction for multiple testing. We propose 'Copy Number estimation by a Mixture Of PoissonS' (cn.MOPS), a data processing pipeline for CNV detection in NGS data. In contrast to previous approaches, cn.MOPS incorporates modeling of depths of coverage across samples at each genomic position. Therefore, cn.MOPS is not affected by read count variations along chromosomes. Using a Bayesian approach, cn.MOPS decomposes variations in the depth of coverage across samples into integer copy numbers and noise by means of its mixture components and Poisson distributions, respectively. The noise estimate allows for reducing the FDR by filtering out detections having high noise that are likely to be false detections. We compared cn.MOPS with the five most popular methods for CNV detection in NGS data using four benchmark datasets: (i) simulated data, (ii) NGS data from a male HapMap individual with implanted CNVs from the X chromosome, (iii) data from HapMap individuals with known CNVs, (iv) high coverage data from the 1000 Genomes Project. cn.MOPS outperformed its five competitors in terms of precision (1-FDR) and recall for both gains and losses in all benchmark data sets. The software cn.MOPS is publicly available as an R package at http://www.bioinf.jku.at/software/cnmops/ and at Bioconductor.","authors":[{"name":"Bodenhofer U"},{"name":"Clevert DA"},{"name":"Hochreiter S"},{"name":"Klambauer G"},{"name":"Mayr A"},{"name":"Mitterecker A"},{"name":"Schwarzbauer K"}],"citationCount":396,"date":"2012-05-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic acids research","title":"cn.MOPS: mixture of Poissons for discovering copy number variations in next-generation sequencing data with a low false discovery rate."},"pmcid":"PMC3351174","pmid":"22302147"}],"topic":[{"term":"Microarray experiment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3518"},{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0080"},{"term":"Sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3168"}],"validated":1},"bioconductor":{"name":"fastseg","version":"1.58.0","license":"LGPL (>= 2.0)","summary":"fastseg - a fast segmentation algorithm","description":"fastseg implements a very fast and efficient segmentation algorithm. It has similar functionality as DNACopy (Olshen and Venkatraman 2004), but is considerably faster and more flexible. fastseg can segment data from DNA microarrays and data from next generation sequencing for example to detect copy number segments. Further it can segment data from RNA microarrays like tiling arrays to identify transcripts. Most generally, it can segment data given as a matrix or as a vector. Various data formats can be used as input to fastseg like expression set objects for microarrays or GRanges for sequencing data. The segmentation criterion of fastseg is based on a statistical test in a Bayesian framework, namely the cyber t-test (Baldi 2001). The speed-up arises from the facts, that sampling is not necessary in for fastseg and that a dynamic programming approach is used for calculation of the segments' first and higher order moments.","